{"qid":"Q183","names":["Federated Republic of Germany"],"title":"Federated Republic of Germany","endonym":{"native":"Deutschland","lang":"de"},"capsule":"The Federal Republic of Germany (Bundesrepublik Deutschland) in its post-reunification form dates from 3 October 1990, when the five reconstituted states of the former German Democratic Republic acceded under Article 23 of the Basic Law; the dataset attests the entity from 1991, following the formal dissolution of the Soviet Union and Germany's full restoration of sovereignty. Reunified Germany became the most populous and economically largest state of the European Union, a founding member of the Eurozone (1999), and a cornerstone of NATO, with Berlin restored as the seat of government in 1999 after the Bundestag's vote to relocate from Bonn. Its political system, anchored in a federal parliamentary democracy with strong constitutional court oversight, has remained stable under successive chancellors from Helmut Kohl through Olaf Scholz.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial extent is precisely and definitively attested through international treaties, with the eastern border confirmed by the Two Plus Four Treaty of 1990.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Germany","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Germany"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Germany","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germany"}],"britannica":"place/Germany","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Germany","rulers":[{"qid":"Q125918433","name":"Helmut Ohl","start":1963,"end":1992,"image":null},{"qid":"Q100798","name":"Heribert Thallmair","start":1969,"end":2002,"image":null},{"qid":"Q1